				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/melatonin0" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1401238); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1401238&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608238255" alt="melatonin0" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1401238)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/melatonin0" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1401238); return false;" >melatonin0</a></span> <span class="odate time_1557806992 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">14 May 2019 04:09</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4248186">
									

<p>Pretty sure I'd seen an earlier version of this at some point. It's obvious that there was a ton of care that has since gone into this.<br />
I really, <em>really</em> fucking adore this piece.</p>
<p>This is the best Lovecraftian SCP on this site, and that's saying something. It's a wonderful exploration of what is essentially a huge slice of the Mythos, and so successfully grounded in reality. The references are there, but very oblique - the submarine exploration mutinying <em>a-la</em> The Temple, etc. I will even bet you edited the eyes in the image of Adler to get that &quot;Innsmouth look.&quot;</p>
<p>This piece does so much of what this site has been lacking lately. It takes a concept/anomaly/whatever and treats it as the Foundation would: cold and head-on. Nothing meta. No -J parading as an anomaly because it's created by POI's/GOI's whose whole thing is making joke anomalies. No interviews that should just be a Tale, wearing an SCP format just to be on the Mainlist.</p>
<p>I know that other Lovecraftian entries have done this, but I wouldn't have hated if one way the Foundation contained this SCP was by normalizing certain themes of it in popular culture. I know it's been done a bunch, but I feel like this entry deserves that device, because I can easily imagine the frustration of artists trying to express their compulsions while being thwarted by the wash of similar concepts treated as fiction. Those dreams can be dismissed because a lot of current popular culture includes some Lovecraftian elements - as inserted by the Foundation, but also likely there by the pervasiveness of <strong>what came before</strong>.</p>
<p>Anyway, if I had any ability to do so, I'd feature this article.<br />
Since I don't - plus one.</p>

